# gui.ges — the vocabulary a GUI program is written in.
#
# **Not part of the core prelude**, for the reason `music.ges` is not: it
# declares constructors, and a constructor's tag is its position, so merging
# it into every program would renumber `Nil`/`Cons` for all of them.  The
# GUI backend prepends it to a GUI program's source instead.
#
# A GUI program supplies `substrate : Sig Sub` — a picture that varies over
# time and says what it listens to.  `gestate/gui.py` drives it: input
# arrives on one channel, and whatever the substrate holds at that instant
# is what gets drawn.

# ── What can be drawn ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Colour := RGB Int Int Int

#: A picture, its extent, and what it listens to — all one tree.
#:
#: **Data rather than a function**, which is what keeps the host's job
#: first-order.  It walks this tree to draw it and accumulates the position
#: on the way down, so when a press arrives it already knows where each
#: element ended up and needs no machinery to find out.
#:
#: **Every element has an extent, and every element is placed by its
#: centre.**  Those two together are what makes layout possible at all: a
#: combinator that has to arrange two things needs to know how big they
#: are, and needs one agreed point on each to line up.  The centre is that
#: point everywhere, with no alignment argument anywhere — `spec/data.md`
#: has the same instinct about one job per name.
#:
#: **The extent is declared, never measured.**  `Rect w h` is `w` by `h`
#: because it says so, not because that is what got painted; `Sized`
#: declares one outright.  A host that inferred extents from the pixels it
#: drew would give `Gap` no size at all, and a layout that changed when a
#: colour changed to the background colour.
Sub := Rect Int Int Colour
     | Circle Int Colour
     | Gap Int Int
     | Over Sub Sub
     | Row Sub Sub
     | Column Sub Sub
     | Shift Int Int Sub
     | Sized Int Int Sub
     | Pad Int Sub
     | TouchX (Chan Float) Sub
     | TouchY (Chan Float) Sub

# ── What can arrive ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# One event type on one channel, rather than a channel per kind of input.
# Channel identifiers are handed out in allocation order, so several
# channels would be positional and fragile; one is also the honest reading
# of what a GUI receives — a single stream of things that happened.

Event := Tick
       | Move Int Int
       | Press Int Int
       | Release Int Int
       | Key Int

input : Chan Event
input = chan

# ── Signals ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `mkSig`, `map`, `scan` and `zipSig` come from `gestate/signal.ges`,
# which the backend prepends before this file.

#: The event stream.  `Tick` before anything has happened, so a program has
#: a value to show at the first instant.
events : Sig Event
events = Tick ::: mkSig (wait input)
# ── The substrate ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# **A substrate is a value, built from smaller ones by ordinary functions**
# — `spec/substrate.md`.  A program supplies one:
#
#     substrate : Sig Sub
#
# the way a synth supplies one `sound`, and what is drawn is what that
# expression says.  Not a registry: nothing appears because of the type its
# declaration happens to have, and nothing is discovered by walking the
# program.  What puts a thing on the canvas is that some expression put it
# there, in the file, where you can read it.
#
# **One level of each name, and it is the one over signals.**  Every
# combinator below takes and returns `Sig`, and there is no second `…Sub`
# spelling of any of them.  The pair used to exist because a helper built
# from numbers it already has is an ordinary function; what it cost was a
# vocabulary twice the size in which the two halves had to be kept in step,
# and a reader had to know which level they were on before they could read
# a line.  `!` is still what joins the two levels, and it lifts over *any*
# number of signals — so the primitives below are one `!` each, and an
# author's own helper is lifted once where it meets a signal.

# ── Colour ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

#: A colour, and one that may vary — which is the point.  A meter that
#: turns red as it clips is one expression, not a second element.
colour : Sig Int -> Sig Int -> Sig Int -> Sig Colour
colour r g b = !RGB r g b

# ── What there is to draw ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Two shapes and a spacer.  Deliberately few: the point is to see layout
# and attachment working, not to be a drawing library.

#: A filled rectangle, `w` by `h`, **placed by its centre** like everything
#: else here.
rect : Sig Int -> Sig Int -> Sig Colour -> Sig Sub
rect w h c = !Rect w h c

#: A filled circle of radius `r`.  Its extent is the `2r` square around it,
#: so a circle in a `row` reserves the room it looks like it occupies.
circle : Sig Int -> Sig Colour -> Sig Sub
circle r c = !Circle r c

#: Room, and nothing drawn in it.  What a layout is padded and spaced with
#: when the space is meant to be somewhere in particular rather than
#: everywhere — `pad` is the everywhere case.
gap : Sig Int -> Sig Int -> Sig Sub
gap w h = !Gap w h

#: Nothing at all, and no room either.  The identity of `over`, `row` and
#: `column` alike, so a fold that builds a substrate has somewhere to
#: start.
blank : Sig Sub
blank = gap 0 0

# ── Putting things together ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Three combinators, one per direction — on top of, beside, below — and
# every one of them lines its two children up **by their centres**.  There
# is no alignment argument anywhere, which is the whole reason a centre is
# the origo: one agreed point on each element and the arranging has nothing
# left to decide.

#: `over a b` draws `a` and then `b`, so **`b` is on top**.  Painter's
#: order, and it is in the program rather than in the file's line order,
#: because the whole point is that composition is something you can read.
#: The extent is the larger of the two, centred.
over : Sig Sub -> Sig Sub -> Sig Sub
over a b = !Over a b

#: `a` to the left of `b`, centres level.  The extent is the two widths
#: added and the taller height.
row : Sig Sub -> Sig Sub -> Sig Sub
row a b = !Row a b

#: `a` above `b`, centres level.  The extent is the two heights added and
#: the wider width.
column : Sig Sub -> Sig Sub -> Sig Sub
column a b = !Column a b

# ── Saying how big something is ──────────────────────────────────────────

#: Declare an extent, whatever the thing inside it happens to be.  The
#: child is centred in the box and is **not** resized — nothing here scales
#: a picture; this says how much room to reserve.  Room larger than the
#: child is a margin, room smaller is a deliberate overlap.
sized : Sig Int -> Sig Int -> Sig Sub -> Sig Sub
sized w h s = !Sized w h s

#: Room on all four sides.  `pad 8 x` is `x` with eight pixels of clearance
#: — the extent grows by sixteen in each direction and the picture does not
#: move relative to its own centre.
pad : Sig Int -> Sig Sub -> Sig Sub
pad n s = !Pad n s

#: Move a whole substrate, picture, attachments and all — and by an amount
#: that may itself vary, which is how a handle follows what it is bound to.
#:
#: **Layout-neutral, and this is the one thing to know about it.**  The
#: extent is the child's, unmoved, so the parent `row` still reserves the
#: room the child declared and the things beside it do not shuffle when
#: this number changes.  That is what makes it usable for a handle inside a
#: fader: the handle slides, the fader stays the size it said it was.  A
#: `moveXY` that dragged the layout with it would make every animation
#: relayout the window.
moveXY : Sig Int -> Sig Int -> Sig Sub -> Sig Sub
moveXY x y s = !Shift x y s

# ── Attachment ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# **The channel goes in.**  An element is bound to what it feeds by an
# ordinary combinator, and what it feeds is a channel the program declared:
#
#     cutoff : Chan Float
#     cutoff = chan
#
#     fader : Sig Sub
#     fader = onTouchY cutoff (rect 40 200 grey)
#
#     level : Sig Float
#     level = 0.5 ::: mkSig (wait cutoff)
#
# The channel travels *inside the structure*, so the host that walks this
# tree to draw it finds the channel it needs at the node that named it — no
# name to resolve, no identifier handed out by allocation order.  The
# program reads it back with `:::` for a behaviour or `wait` for an event,
# which is why there is no `Sub a`: a `Chan` is the source they are both
# derived from.
#
# **What arrives is a fraction of the element's own extent**, from 0 at the
# left or top edge to 1 at the right or bottom, and the host clamps it
# there.  Two things follow, and both were missing before.
#
# *Motion is constrained by construction.*  A fader used to report a raw
# pixel offset, so a hand that kept going carried the handle off the end of
# its own track and there was nowhere in the program to say it should not.
# The bound is now the element's extent, which the element declared, and no
# program has to restate it.
#
# *The number means something without knowing the size.*  `level` is 0 to
# 1 whether the fader is 200 pixels or 40, so the thing it drives is
# written once and the picture is resized without touching it — and the
# same signal drives a synth parameter directly, which is what a control
# value is.
#
# **The channel does not vary and is not a signal.**  It is what this
# element *is* — which channel it feeds — rather than anything it shows,
# and a `Chan` could not go inside a `Sig` in any case: the subgrammar
# keeps channels out of the types signals carry (`spec/data.md` §II.1).
# So only the picture is lifted, with `map` rather than `!` for that
# reason — `!f c s` would lift both arguments and the channel is not one
# to lift.
#
# **One channel per axis, one job per name.**  A fader is `onTouchY` and
# nothing else; a pad is both on one element, which is honest, because a
# pad *is* two parameters and on the audio side would be two knobs.  An
# `Axis` argument would have made every call site carry a constant that
# says which of two functions it meant.

#: Report where a touch is across this element, 0 at the left edge and 1 at
#: the right, clamped.  In the element's *own* extent, so a `moveXY` moves
#: what it hears along with what it draws.
onTouchX : Chan Float -> Sig Sub -> Sig Sub
onTouchX c s = map (p => TouchX c p) s

#: Report where a touch is down this element, 0 at the top edge and 1 at
#: the bottom, clamped.
onTouchY : Chan Float -> Sig Sub -> Sig Sub
onTouchY c s = map (p => TouchY c p) s

# ── Numbers beside a substrate ───────────────────────────────────────────

#: What a bare number means beside a substrate: the same number, always —
#: so `rect 40 200 grey` and `moveXY 60 80 s` read as the sizes and
#: placements they are.  The same instance `audio.ges` gives `Sig Float`,
#: for the type a coordinate has.
instance Num (Sig Int) where
    fromInteger n = constSig n
    (+) a b = zipSig (x y => x + y) a b
    (-) a b = zipSig (x y => x - y) a b
    (*) a b = zipSig (x y => x * y) a b
